## 1. Why layers

### Explain

Imagine one enormous SQL script that reads the CSV files, fixes the typos, converts the dates, removes duplicates, joins everything, and produces the sales report. It works. Then one of these happens:

- The report is wrong and you need to know whether the till sent bad data or your SQL is bad. You can't tell — there's nothing to look at in between.
- A second report needs the same cleaning. You copy-paste it. Now there are two copies of the cleaning logic, and next month one of them gets fixed.
- The till re-sends yesterday's file. Your script has no idea what to do.

Layers exist to give each of those problems a place to live.

```
   LANDING          BRONZE            SILVER              GOLD
   ┌──────┐        ┌────────┐       ┌─────────┐       ┌─────────┐
   │ .csv │ ─────▶ │ exactly│ ────▶ │ clean,  │ ────▶ │ shaped  │
   │ .csv │        │ what   │       │ typed,  │       │ for the │
   │ .csv │        │ arrived│       │ deduped │       │ question│
   └──────┘        └────────┘       └─────────┘       └─────────┘
                        │                 │                 │
                   "what did       "what is true"     "what was
                    they send?"                        asked?"
                        │                 │                 │
                   never edited      one version       many of these,
                   never deleted     of the truth      one per question
```

The rule that makes it work: **each layer may only read from the layer to its left.** Gold never reads bronze. If gold needs something bronze has, the fix is to add it to silver, not to reach past it.

| Layer | Also called | One job |
|---|---|---|
| Bronze | raw, landing, staging | Keep a faithful copy of what arrived |
| Silver | clean, conformed, core | Make it correct and consistent, once |
| Gold | marts, serving, presentation | Answer a specific question fast |

> **Trap.** "Bronze/silver/gold" is not a rule about three databases or three tools. It's a rule about *what a transformation is allowed to do*. You can implement all three as schemas in one DuckDB file — which is exactly what you're about to do.

### Exercise

For each of these, name the layer it belongs in and say why in one sentence.

**(a)** Trimming whitespace from `kid_name`.  
**(b)** A table with one row per kid per month and their total spend.  
**(c)** A copy of the CSV with two extra columns: the filename it came from and the time it was loaded.  
**(d)** Converting `toy_price` from text to `DECIMAL(10,2)`.  
**(e)** Dropping the duplicate R-002 line.

### Solution

**(a) Silver.** Cleaning is silver's job, and doing it once means every gold table downstream gets it for free.

**(b) Gold.** It answers one specific question, at a grain chosen for that question. A different question gets a different gold table.

**(c) Bronze.** The data itself is unchanged — the extra columns are *lineage*, which is bronze's one permitted addition.

**(d) Silver.** Typing is part of "make it correct". Bronze keeps text as text because that's what arrived.

**(e) Silver.** Bronze must keep the duplicate — it's a true record of what the till sent, and you'll want it when someone asks why the till sent it twice.

The pattern in all five: **if it's about what arrived, it's bronze; if it's about what's true, it's silver; if it's about what someone asked, it's gold.**

