## 2. Bronze

### Explain

Bronze is a photograph of what arrived. The temptation is to fix the obvious problems on the way in. Don't. The moment bronze differs from the source file, you have lost the ability to answer "did they send us bad data, or did we break it?"

Bronze may add exactly one kind of thing: **lineage columns** that record where the row came from and when.

```
   sales_2026-08-01.csv                bronze.raw_sales
   ┌────────────────────┐              ┌────────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────┐
   │ R-001,Ana,24.99,2  │  ──────────▶ │ R-001,Ana,24.99,2  │ _source_file │ _loaded_at  │
   └────────────────────┘              └────────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────┘
                                        ▲ every value                ▲ added, never
                                          untouched, still text        derived from data
```

In DuckDB, `read_csv` with `filename=true` gives you the lineage for free. Note `all_varchar=true`: bronze keeps everything as text so a malformed number lands as text rather than failing the load or silently becoming `NULL`.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS bronze;

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE bronze.raw_sales AS
SELECT *,
       now() AS _loaded_at
FROM read_csv('/work/warehouse/landing/*.csv',
              filename    = true,
              all_varchar = true,
              header      = true);
```

| Bronze does | Bronze does not |
|---|---|
| Copy every row and column as-is | Fix typos, trim, upper-case |
| Add `_loaded_at`, `_source_file` | Cast to real types |
| Keep duplicates | Deduplicate |
| Keep rows that look wrong | Filter anything out |

> **Trap.** "Bronze is raw" does not mean "bronze is a dumping ground you never look at". You will look at it — every time a number in gold is disputed, bronze is the evidence.

### Exercise

Create `bronze.raw_sales` from all three landing files. Then answer, in SQL:

**(a)** How many rows landed, and how many came from each file?  
**(b)** Which receipt number appears in two different files?

### Solution

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS bronze;

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE bronze.raw_sales AS
SELECT *, now() AS _loaded_at
FROM read_csv('/work/warehouse/landing/*.csv',
              filename = true, all_varchar = true, header = true);
```

**(a)**

```sql
SELECT filename, count(*) AS rows
FROM bronze.raw_sales
GROUP BY filename ORDER BY filename;
-- .../sales_2026-08-01.csv | 3
-- .../sales_2026-08-02.csv | 2
-- .../sales_2026-08-03.csv | 2
-- total 7
```

**(b)**

```sql
SELECT receipt_no, count(DISTINCT filename) AS files
FROM bronze.raw_sales
GROUP BY receipt_no
HAVING count(DISTINCT filename) > 1;
-- R-002 | 2
```

R-002 arrived on the 1st and was re-sent on the 3rd. **Bronze keeps both.** Seven rows landed, and seven rows is the honest answer to "what did they send us". Fixing it is the next layer's job.

