## 6. Incremental loads

### Explain

Full rebuilds are the right default. You outgrow them when the rebuild takes longer than the gap between runs — and only then.

An incremental load needs two things: a way to identify **new** rows, and a way to handle **late** ones.

```
   Day 1 run          Day 2 run                Day 3 run
   ┌────────┐         ┌────────┐               ┌────────┐
   │ 01.csv │         │ 02.csv │               │ 03.csv │  ← contains a row
   └────────┘         └────────┘               └────────┘    dated day 1
        │                  │                        │
        ▼                  ▼                        ▼
   process all      process only new         process new — but a
                                             high-water mark on
                                             sold_at would MISS the
                                             late day-1 row entirely
```

**The high-water mark trap.** "Load rows where `sold_at` > the newest `sold_at` I've already got" is the obvious approach and it silently drops late-arriving data. The fix is to watermark on **when you received it**, not when it happened:

```sql
-- fragile: misses anything that arrives out of order
WHERE sold_at > (SELECT max(sold_at) FROM silver.sales)

-- robust: based on arrival, not event time
WHERE _loaded_at > (SELECT max(_loaded_at) FROM silver.sales)
```

Even then, incremental silver must still **upsert**, not insert — because a late row may be a correction of one you already have. That's what makes the R-002 duplicate interesting: it isn't new data, it's the same sale sent again.

> **Trap.** Incremental models drift. The full-rebuild result and the incremental result should agree, and over months they stop agreeing for reasons nobody remembers. Schedule a periodic full rebuild and compare — weekly is a common choice.

### Exercise

Your silver load currently rebuilds everything. Write an incremental version that:

**(a)** Only reads bronze rows loaded since the last silver run.  
**(b)** Upserts them into `silver.sales` on `(receipt_no, toy_name)` so a re-sent row updates rather than duplicates.

Then explain in one sentence why `sold_at` would have been the wrong watermark column here.

### Solution

```sql
-- (a) + (b): new arrivals, deduped among themselves, then merged
CREATE OR REPLACE TEMP TABLE new_rows AS
WITH incoming AS (
  SELECT trim(receipt_no) AS receipt_no,
         CAST(sold_at AS TIMESTAMP) AS sold_at,
         trim(kid_name) AS kid_name, trim(kid_city) AS kid_city,
         trim(toy_name) AS toy_name, trim(toy_category) AS toy_category,
         CAST(toy_price AS DECIMAL(10,2)) AS toy_price,
         CAST(quantity AS INTEGER) AS quantity,
         trim(shop_name) AS shop_name, _loaded_at
  FROM bronze.raw_sales
  WHERE _loaded_at > COALESCE((SELECT max(_loaded_at) FROM silver.sales),
                              TIMESTAMP '1900-01-01')
)
SELECT * EXCLUDE (rn) FROM (
  SELECT *, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY receipt_no, toy_name
                               ORDER BY _loaded_at DESC) AS rn
  FROM incoming
) WHERE rn = 1;

MERGE INTO silver.sales AS t
USING new_rows AS s
  ON t.receipt_no = s.receipt_no AND t.toy_name = s.toy_name
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
     sold_at = s.sold_at, kid_name = s.kid_name, kid_city = s.kid_city,
     toy_category = s.toy_category, toy_price = s.toy_price,
     quantity = s.quantity, shop_name = s.shop_name, _loaded_at = s._loaded_at
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT VALUES (
     s.receipt_no, s.sold_at, s.kid_name, s.kid_city, s.toy_name,
     s.toy_category, s.toy_price, s.quantity, s.shop_name, s._loaded_at);
```

**Why not `sold_at`:** the re-sent R-002 row is dated 1 August but arrived on the 3rd, so a watermark on `sold_at` would have judged it older than data already loaded and skipped it — the correction would never land. Arrival time is a property of your pipeline and only ever moves forward; event time is a property of the world and does not.

**Two details worth keeping:** dedupe the incoming batch *before* the merge (a batch can contain two versions of the same key, and `MERGE` errors or picks arbitrarily when the source has duplicate keys), and `COALESCE` the watermark so the very first run — when silver is empty — still loads everything.

