## 8. Conventions and limits

### Explain

**Naming.** Consistency matters more than the specific scheme. A common one:

```
   bronze.raw_<source>              bronze.raw_sales
   silver.<entity>                  silver.sales, silver.kids
   gold.dim_<thing>                 gold.dim_toy, gold.dim_date
   gold.fct_<event>                 gold.fct_sale
   gold.<question>                  gold.daily_category_revenue
```

Prefix or schema — pick one and don't do both. `gold.gold_fct_sale` is what happens when a team decides halfway through.

**Where medallion is overkill.** It's a pattern, not a law:

| Situation | Better |
|---|---|
| One CSV, one report, one person | A single script. Layers add ceremony and no safety. |
| Source is already clean and typed | Bronze and silver collapse into one. Don't invent work. |
| Sub-second serving needs | Gold in a serving store, not the warehouse. |
| Streaming | The layers still apply but the mechanics differ enough that the recipes here don't transfer. |

**Where it earns its keep:** more than one source, more than one consumer, or anyone ever asking "why is this number different from last week?"

> **Trap.** The most common failure isn't skipping a layer — it's letting business logic leak into bronze because it was quicker that time. Once bronze is no longer a faithful copy, it can never again settle the argument about whether the source was wrong, and that was the entire reason it existed.

### Exercise

You inherit a warehouse with these tables. Each breaks one rule from this guide. Name the rule and the fix.

**(a)** `bronze.sales_clean` — trimmed and typed on the way in.  
**(b)** `gold.revenue_report` whose SQL reads from `bronze.raw_sales`.  
**(c)** `silver.sales` built with `INSERT INTO` on a schedule.  
**(d)** Two gold tables that each contain the same forty-line `CASE WHEN` categorising customers.

### Solution

**(a)** Bronze must be a faithful copy. **Fix:** rename it to `silver.sales`, and add a real bronze that lands the file untouched. You may not be able to recover what the original files contained — which is the point of the rule.

**(b)** Layers may only read the layer to their left. **Fix:** whatever gold needed from bronze, add it to silver, then point gold at silver. If gold genuinely needs raw text, that's a signal silver dropped something it shouldn't have.

**(c)** Not idempotent — every run duplicates the table. **Fix:** `CREATE OR REPLACE`, or delete-and-insert by partition inside a transaction.

**(d)** Duplicated logic; the two will diverge the first time one is edited. **Fix:** the categorisation is a property of the customer, not of either report — it belongs in silver as a column, or in an intermediate model both golds read.

